INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7274,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12323025','Shaping local identity through public health: the role of social integration among new-generation rural migrants in urban China.','Wang Y, Xiao C, Zheng Y, Liu Z, Jiang Z, Li Y.','2025','underexplored ols karlson khb khb settle hometowns residences hukou hukou precarious equips prilleltensky mattering rental unconsciously internalize cmds cmds inflows 2010s cmds hukou locals outlander unknowledge unbuild unacceptance provicial provicial communist cmds regressed ols regressed karlson khb decomposes khb khb conciseness ols locals khb associational cmds hukou upbringing dissonance ideologies internalize locals hukou unobserved interoperable cmds proofreading talents equalization enclaves urbanizing ngai huilin unfinished proletarianization peasant ascension returnees chaohu e1967 semiot urbanizing kandilov deaux reconstructing mcareavey e2044 tuner ullah harrigan polychlorinated biphenyls caine mattering scarpa jcop overwork tenures geoforum choguill welf indic entrepreneurs embeddedness entrep eurasian geogr intercult tebboth remaking redevelopment precarity keele tingley peasant outsiders geogr newcomers horiz ritakallio gidron populism hukou e2734 veselova hukou pizzi hukou marzana damia atallah loreto marzana damia pozzi peruvians migr','Abstract Background The health of migrant populations has become a critical public health concern amid China’s rapid urbanization, especially within the framework of the “Healthy China” initiative aimed at reducing health disparities. While existing research has extensively examined the challenges rural migrants face regarding employment, housing, and access to services, the specific role of public health services in shaping local identity remains underexplored. This study investigates how public health influences local identity among new-generation rural migrants and examines the mediating role of social integration in this relationship, addressing a significant gap in the migration and public health literature. Methods Utilizing data from 56,014 new-generation rural migrants, this study employed ordinary least squares (OLS) and logistic regression models to assess the impact of three public health indicators—public health awareness, health records, and health education—on social integration and local identity. The Karlson-Holm-Breen (KHB) method was applied to evaluate mediation effects within a counterfactual framework, adjusting for demographic, socioeconomic, and migration-related variables. Results All three public health variables had statistically significant and positive effects on both social integration and local identity ( p < 0.001). Public health awareness exhibited the strongest association with local identity, followed by health records and health education. KHB decomposition revealed that social integration mediated approximately 30% of the total effect of public health on local identity. Specifically, the mediating effect accounted for 30.22% for public health awareness, 33.12% for health records, and 29.43% for health education. Despite uniformly significant effects, the degree of mediation varied only slightly, indicating a stable and robust mediating role of social integration. Conclusions Public health services are positively associated with the development of local identity among new-generation rural migrants, with social integration serving as a critical mediating mechanism. These findings underscore the potential of comprehensive public health strategies not only to meet the health needs of migrant populations but also to promote their social integration. This research provides empirical support for China’s “Healthy China” initiative and offers actionable insights for designing policies aimed at enhancing migrant inclusion through improved public health access and community-based interventions. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12889-025-24038-8. Keywords: Public health, Local identity, Social integration, New-generation rural migrants Introduction Public health is increasingly recognized as a cornerstone of equitable development and a key strategy for addressing health disparities in rapidly changing societies. This is particularly evident in China’s “Healthy China” initiative, which emphasizes strengthening public health services to narrow regional and population-level disparities in health access and outcomes. A central aim of this initiative is to ensure universal access to basic public health services, including for the substantial population of rural-to-urban migrants residing in cities for over six months [ 1 ]. These services not only alleviate economic and familial burdens but also empower migrants, bolster their confidence in adapting to urban life, and positively influence their intention to settle in cities—ultimately enhancing their overall life satisfaction [ 2 ]. China’s accelerating urbanization has given rise to a growing cohort of new-generation rural migrants—young individuals born after 1980 who differ significantly from earlier generations in terms of education, employment, values, and lifestyle [ 3 – 5 ]. These migrants often navigate between their rural hometowns and urban residences, encountering unique barriers to developing a sense of belonging or local identity. This lack of local identity may adversely affect their psychological well-being, social experiences, and long-term urban integration. While prior studies have explored challenges related to employment, education, housing, language, and access to services [ 6 – 9 ], limited attention has been given to the role that public health services may play in shaping local identity among these migrants. Furthermore, previous research indicates that social integration significantly affects self-reported health outcomes among rural migrants, with observed differences by gender, generation, and income level [ 10 ]. However, little is known about how social integration may influence the formation of local identity, particularly among new-generation rural migrants. The potential mediating role of social integration in the relationship between public health access and local identity formation remains insufficiently investigated. This study aims to address this research gap by investigating the relationship between public health services and the formation of local identity among the new generation of migrant workers and by exploring the underlying mechanisms of this relationship. Literature review Public health and local identity According to constructivist theory, identity formation is a dynamic and continuous process shaped by external influences and individuals’ subjective perceptions of those influences [ 11 ]. For new-generation rural migrants, local identity represents a rational assessment formed within their social group at a psychological level. This process integrates their initial cognitive frameworks with experiences derived from interactions with relevant social groups and exposure to external stimuli [ 12 ]. The concept of the reference group plays a central role in this psychological construction. Reference group theory posits that individuals evaluate their social affiliations and identities based on their perceived membership in, or eligibility for, particular groups [ 13 ]. In the case of new-generation rural migrants in urban areas, two critical reference groups typically emerge: urban residents with local hukou and other migrants with similar rural backgrounds. China’s hukou system determines eligibility for public services such as education, healthcare, and employment opportunities. Migrants who are well-informed about urban public health policies, possess health records, and have received health education occupy a distinctive position. First, awareness of public health policies fosters a sense of equitable treatment compared to urban residents, signaling their recognition as local citizens and easing the psychological construction of local identity [ 14 , 15 ]. Second, compared to less-informed peers, these migrants benefit from broader access to information and public health services, enabling them to utilize formal resources more effectively [ 16 ]. This access strengthens their sense of belonging and transforms their self-perception—from transient residents to active contributors to urban life. Access to public services and socio-economic security is also essential to shaping local identity among new-generation rural migrants [ 17 ]. The evolution of rural-to-urban migration into a family-based endeavor introduces additional challenges—such as childcare, elder care, and the management of major illnesses—that significantly affect migrants’ urban experiences [ 18 , 19 ]. At the same time, migrants face elevated health risks, including infectious diseases, occupational injuries, reproductive health issues, and mental health concerns [ 20 , 21 ]. These risks are compounded by precarious working and living conditions, as well as the lack of social security and support networks in urban settings [ 22 ]. Understanding urban public health systems equips migrants with national health resources that can help them mitigate these risks. This knowledge allows them to reduce concerns over family welfare and healthcare expenses while enhancing their ability to manage health challenges similarly to urban residents. As a result, access to public health services helps bridge social distances and reduces perceived deprivation, thereby fostering stronger local identity among migrant workers [ 15 ]. While existing literature has thoroughly explored the influence of employment, education, and housing on migrants’ local identity, the specific contribution of public health services remains insufficiently addressed. To fill this gap, the present study proposes the following hypotheses: Hypothesis 1a: Awareness of urban public health policies is positively associated with local identity among new-generation rural migrants. Hypothesis 1b: The establishment of health records is positively associated with local identity among new-generation rural migrants. Hypothesis 1c: Receipt of health education is positively associated with local identity among new-generation rural migrants. The mediating effect of social integration The interaction between rural migrants and urban communities plays a critical role in determining both their basic survival and overall quality of life. Scholars have approached this interaction from various theoretical perspectives. Prilleltensky and colleagues [ 23 ] introduced the concept of community mattering, emphasizing individuals’ subjective experiences and emotional value, particularly the dual dimensions of “feeling valued” and “adding value.” In this study, we focus on social integration, defined as migrant workers’ social participation and acceptance within their community. This concept captures the reciprocal adaptation between individuals and their sociocultural environment. Public health services substantially shape migrants’ perceptions of local government responsiveness and their satisfaction with urban public services, thereby influencing their level of social integration and their decisions about whether to remain in urban areas [ 24 , 25 ]. Programs such as public rental housing schemes and urban medical security services meet fundamental needs by offering affordable accommodation and comprehensive health insurance. These initiatives promote the active integration of migrants into urban society by expanding access to resources and enabling meaningful interaction with their environment [ 26 , 27 ]. Public health services play a pivotal role in this process by providing essential health protections that encourage migrants to utilize these services, thereby enhancing their quality of life and deepening their ties to urban communities [ 28 , 29 ]. The establishment of resident health records, which document comprehensive health-related information, further facilitates integration. These records standardize medical service use and ensure accessible documentation, helping migrants navigate urban healthcare systems more efficiently and strengthening their engagement with urban society. Additionally, participation in health education programs and the establishment of health records help bridge disparities in access to public health services between urban residents and rural migrants. These interventions reduce psychological distance and perceptions of marginalization, thereby enhancing migrants’ sense of belonging in the urban environment [ 30 – 32 ]. From the perspective of cultural adaptation, social integration contributes to the formation of local identity through spatial, occupational, and emotional transitions. This process entails a gradual shift from traditional rural norms to the adoption of urban values, behaviors, and social practices—reflecting an ongoing process of adaptation and negotiation [ 33 ]. Through this cultural adaptation, migrants unconsciously internalize the cultural norms of the host community, narrowing perceived boundaries between social groups and reinforcing individual identification with the urban collective [ 34 ]. Successful social integration is associated with an enhanced sense of belonging and a reduction in feelings of inferiority during social interactions [ 35 ]. Social integration also helps mitigate the psychological and social barriers migrants face, reducing experiences of rejection and discrimination while fostering a sense of psychological affiliation with urban communities. As social exclusion diminishes, local identity is strengthened [ 36 – 38 ]. Social networks—built through daily interactions with local residents—play a vital role in bridging the psychological gap between migrants and urban populations. These networks provide both emotional support and practical assistance, facilitating migrants’ acceptance into the host society and contributing to the development of local identity [ 39 , 40 ]. Based on this theoretical foundation, the study proposes the following hypotheses: Hypothesis 2a: Social integration mediates the association between public health awareness and local identity among new-generation rural migrants. Hypothesis 2b: Social integration mediates the association between the establishment of health records and local identity among new-generation rural migrants. Hypothesis 2c: Social integration mediates the association between participation in health education and local identity among new-generation rural migrants. Building upon this theoretical analysis, the conceptual framework illustrated in Fig. 1 depicts how public health influences local identity among new-generation rural migrants, highlighting the mediating role of social integration in this process. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Conceptual framework Methods Data source This study employs data from the 2017 China Migrants Dynamic Survey (CMDS), an annual, large-scale national survey initiated by the National Health Commission in 2009. The CMDS is well-suited for this research due to its broad coverage across 31 provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities, focusing on regions with substantial migrant inflows. With an annual sample size of approximately 200,000 households, the survey provides comprehensive data on demographics, public health access, social integration, and mental health—making it ideal for analyzing the relationships among public health engagement, social integration, and local identity. Following China’s reform and opening-up period, rural-to-urban migration stabilized in the mid-to-late 2010s. To examine the mechanisms influencing local identity among new-generation rural migrants, the most recent officially released dataset—the 2017 CMDS—was utilized. Based on existing literature, new-generation migrants are defined as individuals born in 1980 or later holding rural hukou status. After excluding respondents with missing values for key variables, the final analytical sample comprises 56,014 valid cases. Data analysis was conducted between June and September 2024. By leveraging this nationally representative and most current dataset, the study provides valuable insights for informing public health and urban integration policies targeting migrant populations [ 41 ]. Table 1 summarizes the key descriptive statistics. Among respondents, 71.71% identified as locals, indicating a relatively high level of urban integration. The mean score for social integration was 9.98 (SD = 1.51). Regarding public health engagement, 60.62% reported awareness of basic public health policies, 74.67% had received health education, and only 28.54% had established local health records. Table 1. Descriptive statistics of the variables Variable Categories/Indicators Percentage/Average Local identity Outlander 28.29% Local 71.71% Social integration Mean 9.98 Standard Deviation 1.51 Public health awareness Unknowledge 39.38% Knowledge 60.62% Public health record Unbuild 71.46% Build 28.54% Public health education Unacceptance 25.33% Acceptance 74.67% Age Mean 29.23 Standard Deviation 4.81 Gender Women 46.20% Men 53.80% Nationality Han 89.97% Minority 10.03% Education Primary school and below 7.49% Junior Middle School 46.89% Senior Middle School 26.95% College or above 18.67% Marriage No-partner 25.56% Partner 74.44% Political identity Common people 87.16% CPC member 12.84% Income (logarithm) Mean 8.21 Standard Deviation 0.59 Self-rated health Unhealthy 0.45% Healthy 99.55% Migration range Inter-provicial Migration 50.09% Intra-provicial Migration 49.91% Number of cities 1 49.64% 2 28.26% 3 or above 22.10% Open in a new tab Demographic analysis revealed a mean age of 29.23 years (SD = 4.81), with a gender distribution of 53.80% male and 46.20% female. Most respondents were of Han ethnicity (89.97%), married (74.44%), and had attained junior middle school education (46.89%), with only 18.67% holding a college degree or higher. Politically, 12.84% were members of the Communist Party of China (CPC). The average log-transformed monthly income was 8.21. In terms of migration characteristics, 50.09% had migrated across provinces, while 49.91% had migrated within provinces. Regarding mobility, 49.64% had lived in one city, 28.26% in two cities, and 22.10% in three or more cities. Variables Dependent variable Local identity serves as the dependent variable and is a key indicator of integration. It is measured using Item 503 H from the 2017 CMDS: “I feel that I am already a local.” Responses ranged from “Totally disagree” to “Completely agree” and were dichotomized for analysis: responses indicating disagreement were coded as 0; agreement as 1. Independent variable The independent variable is public health service engagement, measured through the following three indicators: Public health awareness was evaluated by asking respondents whether they were aware of the National Basic Public Health Service Program, with responses coded as 1 for awareness and 0 for lack of awareness. Health records were assessed by inquiring whether respondents had a local community health record; affirmative responses were coded as 1, and negative or uncertain responses were coded as 0. Lastly, health education was evaluated by determining whether respondents had participated in health education on various topics. A value of 1 was assigned for each topic in which the respondent had received education, and 0 otherwise. Mediating variable Social integration is the mediating variable, assessed through three items reflecting emotional connection to the city, concern about urban changes, and willingness to integrate. Responses used a 5-point Likert scale. The items showed strong internal consistency (Cronbach’s α = 0.85) and were combined into a composite score ranging from 3 to 12, with higher scores indicating greater social integration. Control variables This study includes a set of control variables categorized into two groups: (1) Demographic and sociological characteristics, including gender, age, education level, ethnicity, marital status, political affiliation, personal income, and self-rated health status; (2) Migration characteristics, including the extent of mobility (inter- vs. intra-provincial migration) and the number of cities in which the respondent has lived. Analytical strategy To assess the mediating role of social integration, a stepwise regression approach was employed. First, social integration was regressed on the public health indicators using ordinary least squares (OLS), which is appropriate for continuous outcomes. Next, local identity was regressed on public health indicators and social integration using logistic regression due to the binary nature of the dependent variable. The Karlson-Holm-Breen (KHB) method is a statistical technique that decomposes total effects into direct and indirect components in nonlinear models, particularly when dealing with binary outcomes [ 42 ]. Unlike traditional mediation analysis, KHB provides unbiased estimates by comparing nested models with and without mediators. In this study, KHB is especially appropriate because the dependent variable (local identity) is dichotomous, making conventional mediation approaches potentially biased. The method quantifies how much of the effect of public health on local identity operates through social integration versus through direct pathways. In addition, this study conducted robustness checks from three perspectives: the study population, the dependent variable, and the mediation analysis model. For the sake of conciseness, the full details of these robustness tests are reported in the Supplementary Materials. Results Regression results To examine the impact of public health variables on social integration and local identity, six regression models were estimated, as summarized in Table 2 . The independent variables included public health awareness, health records, and health education, along with multiple control variables. Ordinary least squares (OLS) regression was applied to the continuous variable (social integration), while logistic regression was used for the binary outcome variable (local identity). Table 2. Effects of public health on social integration and local identity among new-generation rural migrants Variable Model 1a Model 1b Model 2a Model 2b Model 3a Model 3b Social integration 0.388*** 0.391*** 0.392*** (0.008) (0.008) (0.008) Public health awareness 0.341*** 0.289*** (0.023) (0.021) Public health record 0.338*** 0.278*** (0.027) (0.025) Public health education 0.279*** 0.240*** (0.023) (0.024) Age 0.019*** 0.021*** 0.020*** 0.022*** 0.019*** 0.022*** (0.002) (0.003) (0.002) (0.003) (0.002) (0.003) Men −0.010 0.049** −0.016 0.045** −0.018 0.042* (0.017) (0.022) (0.017) (0.022) (0.017) (0.022) Minority 0.031 −0.027 0.025 −0.030 0.029 −0.029 (0.036) (0.038) (0.035) (0.038) (0.036) (0.038) Education level (Reference group: Primary school or below) Junior middle school 0.097*** 0.049 0.117*** 0.066 0.112*** 0.059 (0.035) (0.042) (0.034) (0.042) (0.034) (0.042) Senior middle school 0.179*** 0.135*** 0.210*** 0.160*** 0.202*** 0.151*** (0.052) (0.045) (0.050) (0.045) (0.050) (0.045) College or above 0.326*** 0.171*** 0.358*** 0.198*** 0.360*** 0.194*** (0.053) (0.049) (0.051) (0.048) (0.052) (0.049) Partner 0.074** 0.038 0.071** 0.036 0.072** 0.036 (0.031) (0.029) (0.030) (0.029) (0.032) (0.029) CPC member 0.093*** −0.182*** 0.098*** −0.179*** 0.100*** −0.177*** (0.023) (0.034) (0.023) (0.034) (0.024) (0.034) Income (logarithm) 0.069** 0.059*** 0.070** 0.059*** 0.072** 0.062*** (0.028) (0.019) (0.028) (0.019) (0.029) (0.019) Self-rated health −0.018 0.601*** −0.007 0.618*** 0.000 0.619*** (0.096) (0.149) (0.097) (0.149) (0.099) (0.149) Intra-provincial migration 0.206*** 0.401*** 0.204*** 0.400*** 0.207*** 0.400*** (0.030) (0.025) (0.030) (0.025) (0.030) (0.025) Number of migration cities (Reference group: 1) 2 0.005 −0.339*** 0.004 −0.341*** −0.000 −0.343*** (0.025) (0.025) (0.026) (0.025) (0.025) (0.025) 3 or above −0.058** −0.606*** −0.061** −0.609*** −0.066*** −0.610*** (0.024) (0.027) (0.024) (0.027) (0.023) (0.027) Provincial fixed effect yes yes yes yes yes yes Constant 8.368*** −5.306*** 8.413*** −5.291*** 8.285*** −5.407*** (0.294) (0.238) (0.294) (0.238) (0.302) (0.238) Observations 56,014 56,014 56,014 56,014 56,014 56,014 R 2 0.159 0.152 0.149 Open in a new tab Standard error for coefficients in brackets *, **, *** means 10%, 5%, and 1% respectively Models 1a, 2a, and 3a demonstrate that all three public health variables—awareness, health records, and health education—have statistically significant and positive effects on social integration ( p < 0.001). These results suggest that new-generation rural migrants who are better informed about public health policies, have established health records, or have participated in health education programs report higher levels of social integration. In Models 1b, 2b, and 3b, the public health variables also exhibit positive and significant associations with local identity ( p < 0.001), indicating that migrants who engage more with public health services are more likely to perceive themselves as “locals.” These findings provide empirical support for Hypotheses 1a–1c, confirming the relevance of public health engagement in fostering local identity. Furthermore, when social integration is introduced into Models 1b, 2b, and 3b, it remains statistically significant, and the coefficients of the public health variables slightly decline but remain significant. This attenuation suggests a partial mediating effect of social integration, providing initial support to Hypotheses 2a–2c. Mechanism and effect decomposition To further investigate the mediating role of social integration in the relationship between public health engagement and local identity, the KHB method was applied to three separate models, each focusing on one public health indicator. Table 3 presents the decomposition of total effects into direct and indirect components. Table 3. Mediation analysis of social integration in the relationship between public health and local identity Effect Model 1 Model 2 Model 3 Total effect 0. 290*** 0.246*** 0.210*** (0.023) (0.026) (0.024) Direct effect 0. 202 *** 0.164*** 0.148*** (0.023) (0.026) (0.024) Indirect effect 0.087 *** 0.081*** 0.062** (0.010) (0.010) (0.010) Mediating effect(%) 30.22 33.12 29.43 Observations 56,014 56,014 56,014 Open in a new tab Standard error for coefficients in brackets *, **, *** means 10%, 5%, and 1% respectively Across all three models, the total, direct, and indirect effects were statistically significant. These results provide additional support for Hypotheses 2a–2c, although they indicate associational rather than causal mediation, given the cross-sectional nature of the data. Among the public health indicators, awareness exerted the strongest total effect on local identity, followed by health records and health education. The indirect effects mediated by social integration accounted for 30.22%, 33.12%, and 29.43% of the total effects, respectively. Despite uniform statistical significance across all public health variables, the proportion of mediation varied only slightly. This consistency suggests that social integration plays a stable and meaningful mediating role in the relationship between public health and local identity among new-generation rural migrants. However, as the analysis is based on cross-sectional data, causal interpretations should be made with caution and would benefit from validation through longitudinal research designs. Discussion The substantial influx of new-generation rural migrants has played a critical role in enhancing community resilience and promoting sustainable urban development, thereby contributing meaningfully to the implementation of China’s “Healthy China” strategy. This study offers a detailed examination of how public health service policies influence the formation of local identity among this population and explores the mediating mechanisms involved, using data from the 2017 China Migrants Dynamic Survey (CMDS). The findings align with the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), particularly the goal of ensuring equitable access to health services, and underscore the need for health systems that are both inclusive and responsive to the evolving needs of diverse urban populations. First, the results indicate that a significant portion of new-generation rural migrants are aware of public health policies and actively participate in health education programs. However, disparities persist—particularly in the establishment of health records. This gap highlights an important area for policy attention. Expanding access to and improving the establishment of health records for migrant populations is essential for promoting inclusive healthcare and ensuring that these individuals can equitably benefit from essential public health services. Second, the findings suggest that public health services may play a meaningful role in supporting both social integration and the development of local identity among new-generation rural migrants. While causality cannot be definitively established in this study, the results underscore the relevance of reference group theory in explaining how migrants come to perceive themselves as members of the urban community. The long-standing urban–rural divide institutionalized by the hukou system has historically generated unequal access to public resources, thereby impeding identification and mutual integration between urban and rural populations [ 43 ]. When migrants perceive that they are receiving equitable treatment—such as access to health services comparable to that of their urban peers—they are more likely to form a stronger sense of local identity and integrate more fully into urban society [ 44 ]. Third, the important role of social integration in shaping local identity is reinforced by our analysis. These findings support the relevance of the cultural adaptation paradigm, which posits that continuous adaptation to new environments is essential for individuals to thrive in unfamiliar settings [ 45 ]. Due to differences in lifestyle, values, and social norms—shaped by rural upbringing, education, and family structures—new-generation rural migrants often experience dissonance with urban counterparts. However, through everyday interactions and immersive experiences, these migrants gradually adopt urban health practices and social norms [ 46 ]. Exposure to contemporary urban lifestyles and ideologies fosters positive attitudes, reinforcing their sense of belonging and identification with urban communities [ 35 , 47 ]. Additionally, the results suggest a potential mediating role of social integration in the association between public health and local identity among new-generation rural migrants. Social integration significantly shapes how migrants interpret and internalize their urban experiences, thereby influencing their subjective sense of belonging [ 46 , 48 ]. Importantly, this identity formation is not imposed but rather emerges through a process of active psychological engagement. Characterized by adaptability and resilience, new-generation rural migrants increasingly define themselves as “locals,” often expressing a willingness to change their hukou status as part of their long-term integration strategy [ 49 – 51 ]. Strengths and limitations This study offers a novel examination of the complex interplay between public health, social integration, and local identity among new-generation rural migrants in China. It provides empirical evidence that familiarity with public health policies, the establishment of health records, and participation in health education are key contributors to promoting social integration. In turn, social integration enhances local identity and increases the likelihood of permanent settlement in urban areas. By revealing the nuanced relationship between public health services and migrants’ sociocultural dynamics, this research contributes a new perspective to the understanding of urban integration within the context of public health discourse. Nevertheless, several limitations should be acknowledged. First, the use of cross-sectional data limits the ability to make causal inferences. Future longitudinal studies are needed to establish temporal order and validate the observed relationships. Second, although the models control for a wide range of covariates, the potential influence of unobserved confounders cannot be entirely ruled out, highlighting opportunities for further investigation. Third, the study relies on data from 2017. While overall migration trends have remained relatively stable, the mediating mechanisms identified may shift in response to evolving policies or changing social contexts. Lastly, although the focus on new-generation rural migrants provides valuable and targeted insights, the findings may have limited generalizability beyond the Chinese context. Expanding future research to include varied sociocultural and economic settings would enhance external validity and offer a broader understanding of these dynamics. Policy implications While prior studies have emphasized the role of community participation and engagement in facilitating migrant integration [ 52 , 53 ], this study provides additional insights for public health policy and urban governance. Enhancing public health awareness is a foundational step toward fostering migrant integration. Policymakers should prioritize culturally responsive and multilingual health education initiatives that are tailored to diverse literacy levels and accessible to all segments of the migrant population. Collaborations with community-based organizations, the use of digital platforms and social media, and the organization of workshops in familiar venues—such as neighborhoods and workplaces—can significantly enhance outreach and engagement. The digitalization of health records through centralized, interoperable databases linked to national identification systems is another critical priority. Offering free initial health assessments and providing healthcare providers with training on digital systems can support successful implementation. Promoting participation in health education through cultural events, workplace-based programs, and attendance incentives—while leveraging local media and communication channels—can further broaden the reach of public health initiatives. Additionally, establishing community hubs that integrate health services with social activities can create shared spaces for interaction and trust-building. Public investment in inclusive community infrastructure, along with training programs for local volunteers to support migrant populations, can reduce social distance, foster a sense of belonging, and facilitate deeper integration into urban communities. Conclusion This study suggests an important association between public health and local identity among new-generation rural migrants in urban China. Using data from the 2017 CMDS, the findings reveal that public health awareness, the establishment of health records, and participation in health education significantly enhance migrants’ local identity. Social integration serves as a critical mediator, linking public health services to the formation of local identity by fostering a sense of belonging and acceptance within urban communities. These insights suggest the potential value of equitable and accessible public health initiatives tailored to the needs of new-generation rural migrants. By addressing health disparities and promoting social engagement, policymakers can strengthen migrants’ local identity, contributing to sustainable urban development and the creation of more inclusive and cohesive urban societies. Supplementary Information Supplementary Material 1. (25.5KB, docx) Acknowledgements Thanks are due to Migrant Population Service Center, National Health Commission P.R. China for sharing their data with us. Authors’ contributions YW took leadership and proposed the analytical framework, participating in the entire writing process of the paper. CX worked on the statistical analysis of the data. YZ and ZL were responsible for collecting and organizing the literature. ZJ was in charge of drafting the initial manuscript and proofreading. YL drafted the concept of the paper and participated in supervising the manuscript. All authors read and approved the final manuscript. Funding This research was funded by New Liberal Arts Research and Reform Practice Project of the Ministry of Education Innovation and Practice Research on Training Compound Talents in Rural Governance (No. 2021100074). Data availability No datasets were generated or analysed during the current study. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate The data for this study, sourced from a public database and approved by China’s National Health Commission’s Mobility Service Center, complied with the ethical guidelines of the 1964 Declaration of Helsinki and later amendments. Written informed consent was obtained from all participants. Consent for publication Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Zhou Z, Jiang Y, Wu H, Jiang F, Yu Z. The age of mobility: can equalization of public health services alleviate the poverty of migrant workers? Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2022;19:13342. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Ouyang W, Wang B, Tian L, Niu X. Spatial deprivation of urban public services in migrant enclaves under the context of a rapidly urbanizing China: an evaluation based on subur